lack` like you did last time.\n37\t-->\n38\t\n39\t**Describe the bug**\n40\t\n41\t<!-- A clear and concise description of what the bug is. -->\n42\t\n43\t**To Reproduce**\n44\t\n45\t<!--\n46\tMinimal steps to reproduce the behavior with source code and Black's configuration.\n47\t-->\n48\t\n49\tFor example, take this code:\n50\t\n51\t```python\n52\tthis = \"code\"\n53\t```\n54\t\n55\tAnd run it with these arguments:\n56\t\n57\t```sh\n58\t$ black file.py --target-version py39\n59\t```\n60\t\n61\tThe resulting error is:\n62\t\n63\t> cannot format file.py: INTERNAL ERROR: ...\n64\t\n65\t**Expected behavior**\n66\t\n67\t<!-- A clear and concise description of what you expected to happen. -->\n68\t\n69\t**Environment**\n70\t\n71\t<!-- Pl\n72\t~~~~\n73\t\n74\t### candidate #8 (score 0.186, open, labels ['C: parser', 'T: bug'])\n75\ttitle: Cannot parse multi-line code split using backslash\n76\t\n77\t~~~~ untrusted\n78\t<!--\n79\tPlease make sure that the bug is not already fixed either in newer versions or the\n80\tcurrent development version. To confirm this, you have three options:\n81\t\n82\t1. Update Black's version if a newer release exists: `pip install -U black`\n83\t2. Use the online formatter at <https://black.vercel.app/?version=main>, which will use\n84\t   the latest main branch.\n85\t3. Or run _Black_ on your machine:\n86\t   - create a new virtualenv (make sure it's the same Python version);\n87\t   - clone this repository;\n88\t   - run `pip install -e .[d]`;\n89\t   - run `pip install -r test_requirements.txt`\n90\t   - make sure it's sane by running `python -m pytest`; and\n91\t   - run `black` like you did last time.\n92\t-->\n93\t\n94\t**Describe the bug**\n95\t\n96\t<!-- A clear and concise description of what the bug is. -->\n97\tWhen my code contains a multi-line expression that's concatenated using backslash (`\\`) and no indentation, black cannot parse my code\n98\t\n99\t**To Reproduce**\n100\t\n101\t<!--\n102\tMinimal steps to reproduce the behavior with source code and Black's configuration.\n103\t-->\n104\t\n105\tFor example, take this code:\n106\t\n107\t```python\n108\tif True:\n109\t    foo = 1+\\\n110\t2\n111\t    print(foo)\n112\t```\n113\t\n114\tAnd run it with these arguments:\n115\t\n116\t```sh\n117\t$ black file.py --target-version py313\n118\t```\n119\t\n120\tThe resulting error is:\n121\t\n122\t> err\n123\t~~~~\n124\t\n125\t### candidate #5 (score 0.181, open, labels ['C: unstable formatting', 'F: fmtoff', 'T: bug'])\n126\ttitle: Unexpected error with Black 25.11.0 when fmt: off and fmt: on are used within block of comments\n127\t\n128\t~~~~ untrusted\n129\t<!--\n130\tPlease make sure that the bug is not already fixed either in newer versions or the\n131\tcurrent development version. To confirm this, you have three options:\n132\t\n133\t1. Update Black's version if a newer release exists: `pip install -U black`\n134\t2. Use the online formatter at <https://black.vercel.app/?version=main>, which will use\n135\t   the latest main branch.\n136\t3. Or run _Black_ on your machine:\n137\t   - create a new virtualenv (make sure it's the same Python version);\n138\t   - clone this repository;\n139\t   - run `pip install -e .[d]`;\n140\t   - run `pip install -r test_requirements.txt`\n141\t   - make sure it's sane by running `python -m pytest`; and\n142\t   - run `black` like you did last time.\n143\t-->\n144\t\n145\t**Describe the bug**\n146\t\n147\t<!-- A clear and concise description of what the bug is. -->\n148\t\n149\t**To Reproduce**\n150\t\n151\t<!--\n152\tMinimal steps to reproduce the behavior with source code and Black's configuration.\n153\t-->\n154\t\n155\tFor example, take this code:\n156\t\n157\t```python\n158\t[\n159\t    (1, 2),\n160\t    # # fmt: off\n161\t    # (3,\n162\t    #    4),\n163\t    # # fmt: on\n164\t    (5, 6),\n165\t]\n166\t```\n167\t\n168\tAnd run it with these arguments:\n169\t\n170\t```sh\n171\t$ black file.py\n172\t```\n173\t\n174\tThe resulting error is:\n175\t\n176\t> 