detection	Render detection log	2026-10-21T18:18:32.8416482Z [engine] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D6A2hxVrJ9tXJ22BPQNXH3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\"items\":[{\"type\":\"add_comment\",\"body\":\"Thanks for the report! This is an automated first-pass triage; a maintainer will make the final call.\\n\\n**Reproduction:** Black 25.11.0, default flags: reformatted, same diff as in the report (the ternary is split over three lines). Black 26.1.0: same output. Black's default safe mode passed, which means it found the result AST-equivalent to the input.\\n\\n**Assessment:** The change only adds line breaks. In Python, a conditional expression has lower precedence than `==`, so `my_var == 999 if abc == \\\"TEST\\\" else 0` already parses as `(my_var == 999) if abc == \\\"TEST\\\" else 0`. When `abc == \\\"TEST\\\"` this gives `assert my_var == 999`, and otherwise `assert 0`. Both the original and the formatted code mean this. The behaviour described as \\\"Original code\\\" would need the parenthesised form `my_var == (999 if abc == \\\"TEST\\\" else 0)`. Black does not add those parentheses, because it never changes the meaning of code. See \\\"Line length\\\" and \\\"Parentheses\\\" in the Black code style docs (black.readthedocs.io).\\n\\n**Next step:** If you see different runtime behaviour before and after formatting, please share a runnable example that shows it. Otherwise this looks like intended behaviour.\\n\\n<sub>black-issue-triage skill v1.0.0 · suggested for maintainers: `R: not a bug`</sub>\",\"temporary_id\":\"aw_sV4NHwIr\"}],\"errors\":[]}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f86164fc-c4fc-476b-bda0-11a86017f753","uuid":"5799163c-872c-44c1-96f4-18c383975a2b","timestamp":"2026-10-21T18:18:28.274Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/gh-aw/threat-detection/agent_output.json","content":"{\"items\":[{\"type\":\"add_comment\",\"body\":\"Thanks for the report! This is an automated first-pass triage; a maintainer will make the final call.\\n\\n**Reproduction:** Black 25.11.0, default flags: reformatted, same diff as in the report (the ternary is split over three lines). Black 26.1.0: same output. Black's default safe mode passed, which means it found the result AST-equivalent to the input.\\n\\n**Assessment:** The change only adds line breaks. In Python, a conditional expression has lower precedence than `==`, so `my_var == 999 if abc == \\\"TEST\\\" else 0` already parses as `(my_var == 999) if abc == \\\"TEST\\\" else 0`. When `abc == \\\"TEST\\\"` this gives `assert my_var == 999`, and otherwise `assert 0`. Both the original and the formatted code mean this. The behaviour described as \\\"Original code\\\" would need the parenthesised form `my_var == (999 if abc == \\\"TEST\\\" else 0)`. Black does not add those parentheses, because it never changes the meaning of code. See \\\"Line length\\\" and \\\"Parentheses\\\" in the Black code style docs (black.readthedocs.io).\\n\\n**Next step:** If you see different runtime behaviour before and after formatting, please share a runnable example that shows it. Otherwise this looks like intended behaviour.\\n\\n<sub>black-issue-triage skill v1.0.0 · suggested for maintainers: `R: not a bug`</sub>\",\"temporary_id\":\"aw_sV4NHwIr\"}],\"errors\":[]}","numLines":1,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01D6A2hxVrJ9tXJ22BPQNXH3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
